<speak>
chapter 9. your job isn’t your worth.
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
as an eldest daughter, you probably learned early that your value came from what you did, not who you were. achievement became survival, and perfectionism felt like the only way to stay safe.
<break time="600ms"/>
that pressure followed you into adulthood: shaping career choices, fueling burnout, and keeping you stuck in roles that reflect duty more than joy. even beyoncé, an eldest daughter herself, admitted:
<break time="600ms"/>
“if i weren’t performing, i’d be a beauty editor, or a therapist. i love creativity, but i also love to help others. my mother was a hairstylist and they listen to everyone’s problems – like a beauty therapist! i can never be safe; i always try and go against the grain. as soon as i accomplish one thing, i just set a higher goal. that’s how i’ve gotten to where i am.”
<break time="600ms"/>
this chapter is about untangling your identity from achievement, examining how family expectations shaped your career, and realigning your work with your authentic self.
<break time="600ms"/>
answer honestly, without overthinking:
<break time="600ms"/>
when did i first learn that love had to be earned through achievement or performance?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> take a moment. pause here, write what comes up, and come back when you're ready. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
who am i when i’m not producing or accomplishing?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> pause here. there's no rush. press play when you're ready to continue. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
what achievements am i chasing that are actually my parents’ dreams or expectations?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> stop the audio for a moment and write your answer. come back whenever you're ready. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
if i already had permission to exist, what would i stop doing at work or in my career?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> pause the audio here, take your time with this, and press play when you're ready. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
perfectionism-as-survival as the eldest sibling, you were always expected to be perfect. report card day was terrifying. even when you gave your best, it never felt like enough. so you pushed harder, 110%, 120%, until striving became survival.
<break time="600ms"/>
perfectionism protected you once. but as an adult, it keeps you trapped. this exercise helps you look at the beliefs perfectionism planted in you, and what it would mean to let them go.
<break time="600ms"/>
fill these in, focusing on work and performance:
<break time="600ms"/>
who taught me that love was temporary?
<break time="600ms"/>
how do i push people away while desperately wanting them to stay?
<break time="600ms"/>
what would i do differently if i believed someone would stay forever?
<break time="600ms"/>
when did being alone become safer than being left?
<break time="600ms"/>
what evidence of permanence would i actually believe?
<break time="600ms"/>
redefining “enough”
